{"problem":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 245\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-chain-title fig-chain-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-chain-title\">Figure 1. Sensory processing chain</title><desc id=\"fig-chain-desc\">The external event is sampled by an accessory structure, transduced into a graded signal, encoded in spikes and processed by central circuits. A response can be lost at any of these stages.</desc><rect x=\"8\" y=\"95\" width=\"124\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Physical</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >stimulus</text><line x1=\"132\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 150 132 L 141.22417438109628 136.79425538604204 L 141.22417438109628 127.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"152\" y=\"95\" width=\"124\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"214\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Accessory</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >structure</text><line x1=\"276\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"294\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 294 132 L 285.22417438109625 136.79425538604204 L 285.22417438109625 127.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"296\" y=\"95\" width=\"124\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"358\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Receptor</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >potential</text><line x1=\"420\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"438\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 438 132 L 429.22417438109625 136.79425538604204 L 429.22417438109625 127.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"440\" y=\"95\" width=\"124\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"502\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Afferent</text><text x=\"502\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >spikes</text><line x1=\"564\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"582\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 582 132 L 573.2241743810963 136.79425538604204 L 573.2241743810963 127.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"584\" y=\"95\" width=\"124\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"646\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Central</text><text x=\"646\" y=\"147\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >processing</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"209\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Stimulus delivery → transduction → transmission → interpretation</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 1. Sensory processing chain.</strong> The external event is sampled by an accessory structure, transduced into a graded signal, encoded in spikes and processed by central circuits. A response can be lost at any of these stages. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","classification":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 240\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-class-title fig-class-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-class-title\">Figure 2. Independent classification axes</title><desc id=\"fig-class-desc\">A Pacinian corpuscle belongs to several categories at once: mechanoreception by stimulus, exteroception by location and an encapsulated primary ending by cellular organisation.</desc><rect x=\"238\" y=\"20\" width=\"244\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"54\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Pacinian corpuscle</text><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"122\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 122 130 L 129.5502303787005 123.4430173685949 L 131.59691751566427 132.81054695700607 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 360 130 L 355.20574461395796 121.22417438109628 L 364.79425538604204 121.22417438109628 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"598\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 598 130 L 588.4030824843358 132.81054695700607 L 590.4497696212995 123.4430173685949 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"17\" y=\"130\" width=\"210\" height=\"80\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"122\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Adequate stimulus</text><text x=\"122\" y=\"184.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Mechanical deformation</text><rect x=\"255\" y=\"130\" width=\"210\" height=\"80\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Location</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"184.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >External/body surface</text><rect x=\"493\" y=\"130\" width=\"210\" height=\"80\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"598\" y=\"165.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Cellular organisation</text><text x=\"598\" y=\"184.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Encapsulated ending</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 2. Independent classification axes.</strong> A Pacinian corpuscle belongs to several categories at once: mechanoreception by stimulus, exteroception by location and an encapsulated primary ending by cellular organisation. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","transduction":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-iv-title fig-iv-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-iv-title\">Figure 3. Reversal of a stimulus-dependent current</title><desc id=\"fig-iv-desc\">For a hypothetical ohmic cation conductance of 2 nS, the current is −120 pA at −60 mV, zero at 0 mV and +40 pA at +20 mV. Opening the same conductance can produce opposite current directions.</desc><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"675\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"425\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"255\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><polyline points=\"65,250 425,150 665,83\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><text x=\"370\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Holding voltage (mV)</text><text x=\"65\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >−60</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >0</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >+20</text><circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"250\" r=\"4\" fill=\"var(--accent)\"/><circle cx=\"545\" cy=\"116.5\" r=\"4\" fill=\"var(--accent)\"/><text x=\"109\" y=\"237\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >−120 pA</text><text x=\"571\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >+40 pA</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >I (pA)</text><text x=\"187\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Inward current below reversal voltage</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 3. Reversal of a stimulus-dependent current.</strong> For a hypothetical ohmic cation conductance of 2 nS, the current is −120 pA at −60 mV, zero at 0 mV and +40 pA at +20 mV. Opening the same conductance can produce opposite current directions. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","potential":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-primary-title fig-primary-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-primary-title\">Figure 4. Primary ending versus separate receptor cell</title><desc id=\"fig-primary-desc\">In the primary ending the graded potential influences the same neuron's trigger region. A mature auditory hair cell instead alters synaptic release onto a separate afferent.</desc><text x=\"95\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Primary ending</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"50\" width=\"180\" height=\"65\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"102\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Receptor ending</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"97\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Graded voltage</text><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"83\" x2=\"262\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 262 83 L 253.22417438109628 87.79425538604202 L 253.22417438109628 78.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"262\" y=\"50\" width=\"180\" height=\"65\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Trigger region</text><text x=\"352\" y=\"97\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Same neuron</text><line x1=\"442\" y1=\"83\" x2=\"512\" y2=\"83\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 512 83 L 503.22417438109625 87.79425538604202 L 503.22417438109625 78.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"512\" y=\"50\" width=\"194\" height=\"65\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"609\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Afferent axon</text><text x=\"609\" y=\"97\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Propagated spikes</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Separate receptor cell</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"180\" width=\"180\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"102\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Hair cell</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Graded voltage</text><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"217\" x2=\"262\" y2=\"217\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 262 217 L 253.22417438109628 221.79425538604204 L 253.22417438109628 212.20574461395796 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"262\" y=\"180\" width=\"180\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Chemical synapse</text><text x=\"352\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Transmitter release</text><line x1=\"442\" y1=\"217\" x2=\"512\" y2=\"217\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 512 217 L 503.22417438109625 221.79425538604204 L 503.22417438109625 212.20574461395796 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"512\" y=\"180\" width=\"194\" height=\"75\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"609\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Afferent neuron</text><text x=\"609\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Propagated spikes</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 4. Primary ending versus separate receptor cell.</strong> In the primary ending the graded potential influences the same neuron's trigger region. A mature auditory hair cell instead alters synaptic release onto a separate afferent. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","threshold":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-dynamic-title fig-dynamic-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-dynamic-title\">Figure 5. Saturating response and dynamic range</title><desc id=\"fig-dynamic-desc\">The model R = 80S/(2 + S) rises steeply at first and approaches a plateau. Half maximum is at S = 2; it is not the detection threshold.</desc><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"370\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Stimulus intensity (model units)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"144\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"14\" transform=\"rotate(-90 20 144)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Response (spikes/s above baseline)</text><polyline points=\"65,245 75,216.42857142857144 85,195 95,178.33333333333331 105,165 115,154.0909090909091 125,145 135,137.30769230769232 145,130.71428571428572 155,125 165,120 175,115.58823529411765 185,111.66666666666666 195,108.15789473684211 205,105 215,102.14285714285714 225,99.54545454545456 235,97.17391304347825 245,95 255,93 265,91.15384615384616 275,89.44444444444446 285,87.85714285714283 295,86.37931034482759 305,85 315,83.70967741935485 325,82.5 335,81.36363636363637 345,80.29411764705881 355,79.2857142857143 365,78.33333333333331 375,77.43243243243242 385,76.57894736842107 395,75.76923076923077 405,75 415,74.26829268292681 425,73.57142857142858 435,72.90697674418601 445,72.27272727272725 455,71.66666666666669 465,71.08695652173913 475,70.531914893617 485,70 495,69.48979591836735 505,69 515,68.52941176470586 525,68.0769230769231 535,67.64150943396226 545,67.22222222222223 555,66.81818181818184 565,66.42857142857142 575,66.05263157894737 585,65.68965517241381 595,65.33898305084745 605,65 615,64.672131147541 625,64.35483870967741 635,64.04761904761904 645,63.75 655,63.46153846153845 665,63.18181818181816\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >2</text><text x=\"49\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >40</text><text x=\"49\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >80</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >20</text><text x=\"435\" y=\"67\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Saturation</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 5. Saturating response and dynamic range.</strong> The model R = 80S/(2 + S) rises steeply at first and approaches a plateau. Half maximum is at S = 2; it is not the detection threshold. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","intensity":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-rate-title fig-rate-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-rate-title\">Figure 6. Equal counts, different temporal patterns</title><desc id=\"fig-rate-desc\">The two spike trains each contain ten impulses within a one-second window. An onset burst and a sustained pattern have equal average rate but distinct timing.</desc><text x=\"360\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >One-second observation window</text><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"148\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"148\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"264\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"264\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"322\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"322\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"380\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"438\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"438\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"496\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"496\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"554\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"554\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"612\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"612\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"360\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >A: ten spikes in an onset burst</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >B: ten spikes distributed over time</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >0 s</text><text x=\"680\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >1 s</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 6. Equal counts, different temporal patterns.</strong> The two spike trains each contain ten impulses within a one-second window. An onset burst and a sustained pattern have equal average rate but distinct timing. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","adaptation":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-adaptation-title fig-adaptation-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-adaptation-title\">Figure 7. Phasic and tonic responses to a maintained step</title><desc id=\"fig-adaptation-desc\">The same sustained stimulus produces a transient response in the phasic model and a nonzero maintained response in the tonic model. Offset transients are possible but not compulsory.</desc><text x=\"360\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Constant stimulus step</text><polyline points=\"70,65 180,65 180,35 550,35 550,65 675,65\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"675\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><polyline points=\"70,145 180,145 180,88 200,120 230,139 550,145 550,105 565,135 590,145 675,145\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><text x=\"355\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Rapidly adapting / phasic</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"255\" x2=\"675\" y2=\"255\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><polyline points=\"70,255 180,255 180,178 220,200 300,215 550,215 550,255 675,255\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><text x=\"360\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Slowly adapting / tonic</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Onset</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Offset</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 7. Phasic and tonic responses to a maintained step.</strong> The same sustained stimulus produces a transient response in the phasic model and a nonzero maintained response in the tonic model. Offset transients are possible but not compulsory. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","fields":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-fields-title fig-fields-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-fields-title\">Figure 8. Overlapping receptive fields and population localisation</title><desc id=\"fig-fields-desc\">The same point can activate two overlapping fields; relative activity can shift with position. The field boundary is operational, not a rigid anatomical wall.</desc><circle cx=\"245\" cy=\"145\" r=\"105\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"425\" cy=\"145\" r=\"105\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 5\"/><circle cx=\"325\" cy=\"145\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"425\" cy=\"145\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Field A</text><text x=\"480\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Field B</text><text x=\"325\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >P</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Q</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Compare the relative response to positions P and Q.</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 8. Overlapping receptive fields and population localisation.</strong> The same point can activate two overlapping fields; relative activity can shift with position. The field boundary is operational, not a rigid anatomical wall. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","labelled":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-lines-title fig-lines-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-lines-title\">Figure 9. Pathway identity and modality</title><desc id=\"fig-lines-desc\">Identical spike counts can convey different modalities because visual and tactile inputs reach distinct central pathways.</desc><rect x=\"25\" y=\"38\" width=\"180\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"66.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Photoreceptive</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"85.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >pathway</text><line x1=\"205\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 510 70 L 501.22417438109625 74.79425538604202 L 501.22417438109625 65.20574461395798 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"255\" y1=\"49\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"49\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"49\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"390\" y1=\"49\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"435\" y1=\"49\" x2=\"435\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><rect x=\"510\" y=\"38\" width=\"185\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"602.5\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Visual processing</text><rect x=\"25\" y=\"165\" width=\"180\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"193.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Cutaneous</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"212.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >afferent</text><line x1=\"205\" y1=\"197\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 510 197 L 501.22417438109625 201.79425538604204 L 501.22417438109625 192.20574461395796 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"255\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"390\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"435\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"435\" y2=\"197\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><rect x=\"510\" y=\"165\" width=\"185\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"602.5\" y=\"203\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Tactile processing</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Same number of spikes; different circuit identity</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 9. Pathway identity and modality.</strong> Identical spike counts can convey different modalities because visual and tactile inputs reach distinct central pathways. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","inhibition":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-edge-title fig-edge-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-edge-title\">Figure 10. Contrast enhancement at a spatial edge</title><desc id=\"fig-edge-desc\">The illustrative input levels are 20 and 60. With nearest-neighbour weight 0.25 the interior outputs are 10 and 30, but the edge outputs are 0 and 40. The border stands out relative to the interiors.</desc><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"370\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Channel position across the edge</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"144\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"14\" transform=\"rotate(-90 20 144)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Equivalent response units</text><polyline points=\"105,185 235,185 365,185 495,65 625,65\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/><polyline points=\"105,215 235,215 365,245 495,125 625,155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><text x=\"185\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Dashed: input</text><text x=\"523\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Solid: inhibited output</text><text x=\"365\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Edge −</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Edge +</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Interior</text><text x=\"625\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Interior</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 10. Contrast enhancement at a spatial edge.</strong> The illustrative input levels are 20 and 60. With nearest-neighbour weight 0.25 the interior outputs are 10 and 30, but the edge outputs are 0 and 40. The border stands out relative to the interiors. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-surround-title fig-surround-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-surround-title\">Figure 11. Centre-surround antagonism</title><desc id=\"fig-surround-desc\">An ON-centre, OFF-surround field responds most strongly to light restricted to the centre. Uniform light stimulates both opposing regions, so the net response can be smaller.</desc><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"145\" r=\"105\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"145\" r=\"49\" fill=\"var(--surface)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >ON +</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >OFF −</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >OFF −</text><rect x=\"370\" y=\"45\" width=\"310\" height=\"70\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"525\" y=\"75.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Centre-only light</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"94.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Strong excitation</text><rect x=\"370\" y=\"170\" width=\"310\" height=\"70\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"525\" y=\"200.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Centre + surround</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"219.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Partial cancellation</text><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 370 80 L 363.4663138667633 87.57039929675776 L 360.0995629647618 78.59239689142036 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"205\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 370 205 L 360.19364366366585 206.95841144932294 L 363.0536566483932 197.8063698981955 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 11. Centre-surround antagonism.</strong> An ON-centre, OFF-surround field responds most strongly to light restricted to the centre. Uniform light stimulates both opposing regions, so the net response can be smaller. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","psychophysics":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-psych-title fig-psych-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-psych-title\">Figure 12. Detection in a two-alternative forced-choice task</title><desc id=\"fig-psych-desc\">At low intensity performance approaches the 50% guessing baseline. A stated 75% correct criterion defines an operational threshold; the curve is probabilistic, not a hard step.</desc><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><text x=\"370\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Stimulus amplitude (arbitrary units)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"144\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"14\" transform=\"rotate(-90 20 144)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Correct choices (%)</text><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"225\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"225\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 5\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"680\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 5\"/><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 5\"/><polyline points=\"65,224 125,221 185,210 245,190 300,160 360,135 420,100 480,71 540,54 610,47 680,45\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" /><text x=\"47\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >50</text><text x=\"47\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >75</text><text x=\"47\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >100</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Threshold</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >75% criterion</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 12. Detection in a two-alternative forced-choice task.</strong> At low intensity performance approaches the 50% guessing baseline. A stated 75% correct criterion defines an operational threshold; the curve is probabilistic, not a hard step. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>","experiments":"<figure class=\"figure-panel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fig-tests-title fig-tests-desc\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;color:var(--ink)\"><title id=\"fig-tests-title\">Figure 13. Locating a sensory deficit with complementary measurements</title><desc id=\"fig-tests-desc\">Natural stimulation tests the full peripheral chain; current injection bypasses the transduction step. Voltage-clamp and spike recordings distinguish possible sites of failure.</desc><rect x=\"15\" y=\"28\" width=\"180\" height=\"62\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Natural stimulus</text><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"59\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 265 59 L 256.22417438109625 63.79425538604203 L 256.22417438109625 54.20574461395797 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"265\" y=\"28\" width=\"180\" height=\"62\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"355\" y=\"54.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Transduction</text><text x=\"355\" y=\"73.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >current</text><line x1=\"445\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"515\" y2=\"59\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 515 59 L 506.22417438109625 63.79425538604203 L 506.22417438109625 54.20574461395797 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"515\" y=\"28\" width=\"190\" height=\"62\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Spike output</text><rect x=\"15\" y=\"176\" width=\"180\" height=\"62\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Injected current</text><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"207\" x2=\"515\" y2=\"207\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" /><path d=\"M 515 207 L 506.22417438109625 211.79425538604204 L 506.22417438109625 202.20574461395796 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"515\" y=\"176\" width=\"190\" height=\"62\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"var(--panel)\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"610\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Spike output</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Bypass receptor transduction</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" >Normal injected-current response localises the defect upstream of tested excitability.</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 13. Locating a sensory deficit with complementary measurements.</strong> Natural stimulation tests the full peripheral chain; current injection bypasses the transduction step. Voltage-clamp and spike recordings distinguish possible sites of failure. All numerical plots are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>"}